      "decisionSummary": "Four species are recognized in the Larus argentatus complex based on a combination of genetics, plumage, and vocalizations: polytypic L. argentatus (including argenteus); monotypic L. smithsonianus; monotypic L. vegae; and monotypic L. mongolicus. Mitochondrial and nuclear DNA data consistently support separation of L. smithsonianus from L. argentatus (Liebers et al. 2004; Černý & Natale 2022; Sternkopf et al. 2010; Sonsthagen et al. 2016), an arrangement also supported by differences in adult and juvenile plumage, and apparently vocalizations. Species L. vegae is sometimes considered a subspecies of L. smithsonianus but differs phenotypically at a level more consistent with species status in this complex. Finally, L. mongolicus was previously placed in L. cachinnans but is phenotypically distinct from that species and from L. vegae with which it is sometimes aligned; its vocalizations also appear to be the most distinctive of the species formerly placed in L. argentatus. A comprehensive genomic DNA analysis would be informative given the complex demographic and biogeographic history of this group.",
