      "decisionSummary": "Leucocarbo bougainvillii has also been used. The Code article that applies here is 32.5.1: \"…If there is in the original publication itself, without recourse to any external source of information, clear evidence of an inadvertent error, such as a lapsus calami or a copyist's or printer's error, it must be corrected. Incorrect transliteration or latinization, or use of an inappropriate connecting vowel, are not to be considered inadvertent errors…\". This article has consistently been interpreted by the zoological community over the last few decades that mistakes in declension endings (gender or singular/plural) must be corrected IF (and only if) the original description expressly states who the name is dedicated to. Over the decades, countless names have been corrected under this article from a masculine ending in \"-i\" or \"-ii\" to a feminine ending in \"-ae\" if it turned out that the honoree was a woman. In this case, the ending must be corrected to plural as it turns out that the name is dedicated to two Bougainvilles, not one.",
